For correctness, assume every operation that could remove an on-call doctor follows this protocol, with no bypassing updates or deletions. In any serial execution, the first successful departure changes the count from 2 to 1; the next transaction sees 1 and makes no update. PostgreSQL serializable isolation ensures committed transactions are equivalent to a serial execution, aborting an attempt when necessary, so concurrent committed departures cannot leave zero doctors on call. Retrying the whole transaction preserves that reasoning; serializable isolation guarantees safety, not that every attempt succeeds immediately.

