Exact-once coverage proof: those sixteen initial logical groups partition the nineteen inputs. After the stage for bit 1, each logical rank holds its aligned two-group sum; after bits 2, 4, and 8 it holds its aligned four-, eight-, and sixteen-group sum. At each stage the two operand groups are disjoint, so no input is duplicated. Lanes 16–18 maintain copies of logical ranks 0–2, with the same operand order, but butterfly sources always name physical lanes 0–15. Therefore those copies never become additional inputs, and all nineteen callers finish with the same complete addition tree.

Participation proof: the first shuffle reads either the paired lane or self, always within lanes 0–18. An unpaired lane still executes that shuffle but does not add its self-read value. Every subsequent source is `rank ^ bit`, which lies in 0–15 and participates. All nineteen callers execute the same five collectives with the same mask and width 32; only the source lane varies. The synchronized collectives provide the required rendezvous without assuming lockstep execution.

