4. Thread zero alone writes the output, using ascending-index, round-to-nearest float additions starting from zero. For `valid == 0`, it reads no shared-memory entries and writes `0.0f` itself.
5. Stream ordering places the input copy before the kernel and the output copy after it. The host keeps the pinned input unchanged and waits for the final event before reading the output or freeing any buffers.

