For iteration i, the ready event establishes P_i → C_i. For slot reuse at iteration i+2, the consumed event establishes C_i → P_(i+2). Thus each consumer reads after its own producer finishes and before the next producer overwrites that slot. All waits capture already-submitted records, so rerecording does not change their dependencies; first use is safe without relying on an unrecorded event. The dependency chain moves forward through iterations and introduces no cycle. Successful launch checks alone cover immediate launch errors; the final stream checks also check completion errors.

PostgreSQL repeatable read permits write skew: both transactions can commit and leave nobody on call. Serializable isolation prevents that outcome, provided every transaction changing this invariant follows the protocol and serialization failures trigger a retry of the entire transaction.

An exact repeatable-read execution starts with Alice=true and Bob=true:
1. Alice’s transaction begins and counts two on-call doctors, establishing its snapshot.
2. Bob’s transaction begins and also counts two, before either update.
3. Alice’s transaction sets Alice=false. Its own view now has Bob still on call.
4. Bob’s transaction sets Bob=false. Its snapshot still has Alice on call.
5. Alice’s transaction commits.
6. Bob’s transaction commits. The database now has Alice=false and Bob=false.

Updating different rows matters because there is no competing update to the same row to force a write conflict. Repeatable read preserves each transaction’s snapshot, but does not prevent this cross-row read/write dependency cycle.

