    SELECT count(*) INTO available
    FROM doctors
    WHERE on_call;

    IF available > 1 THEN
        UPDATE doctors
        SET on_call = false
        WHERE name = who;
    END IF;
END;
$$;
```

Then execute these in two separate PostgreSQL sessions, concurrently:
```sql
-- Session 1
BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;
SELECT leave_call('Alice');
COMMIT;
```
```sql
-- Session 2
BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;
SELECT leave_call('Bob');
COMMIT;
```

If either statement or COMMIT reports SQLSTATE 40001, roll back as needed and retry from BEGIN in a new transaction: repeat the count, the conditional decision, the update if appropriate, and COMMIT. Do not retry only the update or reuse the earlier count; the retry must use a fresh snapshot. The SQL above is one attempt per session; the caller must implement that whole-transaction retry loop.

For correctness, every serial execution preserves the invariant: starting with at least one on-call doctor, a transaction with count at most one changes nothing; a transaction with count greater than one removes at most one on-call doctor and leaves at least one. PostgreSQL serializable isolation makes successfully committed transactions equivalent to a serial execution, aborting a transaction when necessary rather than allowing both conflicting decisions to commit. After one doctor leaves, a fresh retry sees only one on-call doctor and leaves that doctor on call; this guarantee assumes no other update or deletion bypasses the invariant-preserving protocol.

